### API surface sketch

Your API surface should separate **business APIs**, **workflow APIs**, and **platform APIs**. FastAPI naturally supports grouped routers, so this split helps keep the frontend simple while preserving domain ownership in backend code. citeturn8search6turn10view5

A strong initial API sketch is:

```text
GET    /api/v1/goals
POST   /api/v1/goals
GET    /api/v1/projects
POST   /api/v1/tasks
PATCH  /api/v1/tasks/{task_id}

GET    /api/v1/career/applications
POST   /api/v1/career/applications
POST   /api/v1/career/resume-versions

POST   /api/v1/documents/upload
POST   /api/v1/documents/{id}/ingest
GET    /api/v1/search?q=...
POST   /api/v1/retrieval/query

GET    /api/v1/memory/facts
POST   /api/v1/memory/facts
POST   /api/v1/memory/episodes

POST   /api/v1/agents/run
GET    /api/v1/workflows/{workflow_id}
POST   /api/v1/workflows/{workflow_id}/resume
POST   /api/v1/approvals/{approval_id}

GET    /api/v1/events
GET    /api/v1/evals/runs
POST   /api/v1/evals/replay/{run_id}
```

The important principle is that `/agents/run` and `/workflows/*` are **process endpoints**, while `/goals`, `/tasks`, `/applications`, and `/memory/*` are **state endpoints**. That distinction becomes more valuable as you add orchestration, retries, approvals, and eval replays. LangGraph’s persistence and interrupts make this workflow/state split especially natural. citeturn9view10turn5search13

### Agent registry specification

A registry is worth adding early because tool and agent sprawl is one of the easiest ways agent systems become unmaintainable. MCP, Semantic Kernel plugins, and plugin-based function calling all point toward the same architectural lesson: **capabilities should be described with metadata and schemas, not hard-coded branching logic**. citeturn9view14turn10view1turn10view2turn10view4

A good registry record should look like this:

```yaml
agent_id: career_advisor
version: 1.0.0
domain: career
kind: subgraph
description: Advises on applications, positioning, and resume tailoring.
entrypoint: orchestration.subgraphs.career:graph
input_schema_ref: schemas/career_advisor_input.json
output_schema_ref: schemas/career_advisor_output.json
required_tools:
  - jobs.search
  - docs.retrieve
  - profile.read
optional_tools:
  - email.draft
required_memories:
  - user_profile
  - career_facts
retrieval_policy:
  domains: [career, knowledge, memory]
  top_k: 12
  rerank: true
approval_policy:
  mode: human_required_for_external_send
limits:
  max_steps: 12
  timeout_seconds: 90
observability:
  trace_tags:
    - domain:career
    - agent:career_advisor
safety:
  allowed_actions:
    - read
    - analyze
    - draft
```

This registry should exist for **tools** as well. Anthropic’s guidance on tool design emphasizes namespacing, ergonomic tool specifications, meaningful context in responses, and evaluation of tool quality. That is exactly why a registry should be treated as a product surface, not a config afterthought. citeturn11search3

### Event schema examples

